he amount of DNA in a cell obtained at the end of mitosis is 3.3 a.u., then", "options": ["the amount of DNA in this cell is 6.6 a.u. during G₁ of Interphase and 3.3 a.u. during G₂.", "the amount of DNA in this cell is 6.6 a.u. during G₁ and during S phase of interphase.", "the amount of DNA in this cell is 3.3 a.u. during G₁ and end of S phase of interphase.", "the amount of DNA in this cell is 3.3 a.u. during G₂ and during S phase of interphase.", "the amount of DNA in this cell is 6.6 a.u. during G₂ of Interphase and 3.3 a.u. during G₁."], "answer": 4, "explanation": "", "source": "EST New 5", "hasVisual": false, "visualNote": null}, {"id": "est-new-5-q35", "text": "(Table) Referring to the above table, which fatty acid(s) is/ are saturated fatty acid(s)?", "options": ["Linoleic acid only", "Oleic acid only", "Stearic acid only", "Linoleic acid and oleic acid", "Stearic acid, oleic acid, and linoleic acid"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "", "source": "EST New 5", "hasVisual": true, "visualNote": null, "visualMermaid": "graph LR\n    S[\"Stearic acid: 0 double bonds\"] --> Sat[\"Saturated\"]\n    O[\"Oleic acid: 1 double bond\"] --> Mono[\"Monounsaturated\"]\n    L[\"Linoleic acid: 2 double bonds\"] --> Poly[\"Polyunsaturated\"]\n"}, {"id": "est-new-5-q36", "text": "A scientist covered only the upper side of a plant's leaves with a transparent layer and kept the lower side intact. How would this affect the plant?", "options": ["The plant will not undergo photosynthesis.", "The rate of photosynthesis done by the plant will increase.", "The gas exchange through the stomata will stop since the upper side of the leaves has more stomata than the lower side.", "The gas exchange through the stomata will stop since the upper side of the leaves has fewer stomata than the lower side.", "The gas exchange through the stomata will barely decrease since the lower side of the leaves has more stomata than the upper side."], "answer": 4, "explanation": "", "source": "EST New 5", "hasVisual": false, "visualNote": null}, {"id": "est-new-5-q37", "text": "When round pea plants are crossed with wrinkled pea plants, the resulting offspring plants exhibit only round seeds. What conclusion can be derived from this cross?", "options": ["The round seed characteristic is dominant, and the parent with the round peas is hybrid, while the parent with the wrinkled peas is pure.", "The round seed characteristic is dominant, and the parent with the round peas is pure, while the parent with the wrinkled peas is hybrid.", "The round seed characteristic is dominant, and both parents are pure.", "The round seed characteristic is dominant, and both parents are hybrids.", "The round seed characteristic is recessive, and both parents are pure."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "", "source": "EST New 5", "hasVisual": false, "visualNote": null}, {"id": "est-new-5-q38", "text": "(Table) What can you conclude concerning the effect of enzyme concentration on the speed of reaction?", "options": ["When the enzyme concentration increases, the reaction rate becomes slower.", "The optimal reaction rate occurs when the enzyme concentration is at 5%.", "When the enzyme concentration increases, the reaction rate becomes faster.", "The optimal reaction rate occurs when the enzyme concentration is at 10%.", "The reaction rate is not affected by the enzyme concentration."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "", "source": "EST New 5", "hasVisual": true, "visualNote": null, "visualMermaid": "graph LR\n    E5[\"5% Enzyme -> 0.5 a.u.\"] --> E10[\"10% Enzyme -> 1.5 a.u.\"]\n    E10 --> E15[\"15% Enzyme -> 2.5 a.u.\"]\n    E15 --> E20[\"20% Enzyme -> 3.5 a.u.\"]\n    Trend[\"Positive linear correlation: Increasing enzyme conc. increases reaction speed\"]\n    E5 & E10 & E15 & E20 --> Trend\n"}, {"id": "est-new-5-q39", "text": "Which of the following statements best describes the role of stomata?", "options": ["It absorbs sunlight.", "It is the site where photosynthesis occurs.", "It absorbs water and minerals from the soil.", "It is the site of the gas exchange.", "It transports sugars and minerals."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "", "source": "EST New 5", "hasVisual": false, "visualNote": null}, {"id": "est-new-5-q40", "text": "Proteins that lower the activation energy of a chemical reaction are", "options": ["enzymes", "hormones", "antigens", "immunoglobulin", "receptors"], "answer": 0, "explanation": "", "source": "EST New 5", "hasVisual": false, "visualNote": null}, {"id": "est-new-5-q41", "text": "The process by which bacteria in nature convert nitrogen gas into ammonia is", "options": ["denitrification", "nitrification", "nitrogen fixation", "chemosynthesis", "nutrient limitation"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "", "source": "EST New 5", "hasVisual": false, "visualNote": null}, {"id": "est-new-5-q42", "text": "The first organisms in a food chain are", "options": ["producers", "herbivores", "carnivores", "omnivores", "detritivores"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "", "source": "EST New 5", "hasVisual": false, "visualNote": null}, {"id": "est-new-5-q43", "text": "(Graph) What conclusion can be drawn concerning the amount of CO₂ in the atmosphere?", "options": ["The atmospheric CO₂ started increasing in the year 1000, and it reached its peak in year 2000.", "The atmospheric CO₂ started increasing in the year in the 1000, and it reached its peak in year 1900.", "The atmospheric CO₂ started increasing in the year 1500, and it reached its peak in year 2000.", "The atmospheric CO₂ started increasing in the year 1700, and it reached its peak in year 2000.", "The atmospheric CO₂ started increasing in the year 1850, and it reached its peak in year 2000."], "answer": 4, "explanation": "", "source": "EST New 5", "hasVisual": true, "visualNote": null, "visualSvg": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 450 180\" width=\"100%\" height=\"180\">\n  <line x1=\"45\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <line x1=\"45\" 